---
import { site } from '@/data/site';
import ArticleLayout from '@/layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';

const published = new Date('2026-10-04');
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'When is a double torsion spring better than two single ones?',
    a: 'When both sides of a hinge must push together and you want the torque balanced on one pin. One centre loop, one part number.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do you make them?',
    a: `Yes. Double torsion is our second-highest volume, after single torsion. Wire ${site.capability.wireRangeLabel}.`,
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
  route="whatIsDoubleTorsion"
  title="What Is Double Torsion"
  description="A double torsion spring is two opposite-wound coils joined by a centre loop. When to use one, and what to put on the drawing."
  h1="What is a double torsion spring?"
  lede="Two coils, wound opposite ways, joined by a centre loop that sits over the hinge pin. Both sides of the hinge can push together."
  published={published}
  context="a double torsion spring"
  takeaways={[
    'Two opposite-wound coils, one centre loop.',
    'Used when a hinge needs balanced torque on both sides.',
    'The drawing must give both legs, the loop width, wind and the shaft.',
  ]}
  toc={[
    { id: 'what', text: 'The layout' },
    { id: 'when', text: 'When to use it' },
    { id: 'drawing', text: 'What to put on the drawing' },
    { id: 'questions', text: 'Questions' },
  ]}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={[
    {
      to: 'doubleTorsion',
      label: 'Double torsion springs',
      description: 'What we make, and the spec table.',
    },
    { to: 'torsion', label: 'Torsion springs', description: 'One coil, two legs.' },
    {
      to: 'typesCompared',
      label: 'Torsion vs extension vs compression',
      description: 'Which motion you have.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <h2 id="what">The layout</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>double torsion spring</strong> is two helical coils on one piece of wire. They are
    wound opposite ways so that when the legs move, both coils wind up (or both unwind) together. A{' '}
    <a href="/resources/spring-glossary/#centre-loop">centre loop</a> joins them and usually sits
    over the hinge pin.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is not two springs loosely paired. It is one part. It is also not a spiral spring, which is
    wound flat.
  </p>

  <h2 id="when">When to use it</h2>
  <p>
    A lid, a clamp, a switch or a fold where both sides of the pin must push. A single torsion
    spring on one side leaves the hinge unbalanced. Two separate torsion springs mean two part
    numbers and twice the chance the winds do not match.
  </p>
  <p>
    In our unit, double torsion is the second-largest volume after single torsion. Wire{' '}
    {site.capability.wireRangeLabel}.
  </p>

  <h2 id="drawing">What to put on the drawing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Wire diameter and mean or outer diameter of each coil</li>
    <li>Coils on each side (they are often the same)</li>
    <li>Centre-loop width, and the shaft or pin it sits on</li>
    <li>Both leg lengths and shapes</li>
    <li>Free angle and the working angle, with torque if you have it</li>
    <li>Wind direction for each coil (they should be opposite)</li>
    <li>Material and quantity</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Send the drawing or an unused sample. We quote within 24 hours.</p>
</ArticleLayout>
